        "id": "p17-q94-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that if $n$ is even, then $$\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)=\\sin\\theta.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q94-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)=\\sin\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q94-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Even ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " adds an integer number of full revolutions.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Write $n=2k$, where $k$ is an integer. Full revolutions leave the terminal ray unchanged.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)&=\\sin(360k^{\\circ}+\\theta)\\\\\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)&=\\sin\\theta.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q94-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that if $n$ is odd, then $$\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q94-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q94-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Odd ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " adds full revolutions and one half-turn; ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin(180^{\\circ}+\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Write $n=2k+1$, where $k$ is an integer. Remove the full revolutions, then use the third-quadrant sine identity.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)&=\\sin(360k^{\\circ}+180^{\\circ}+\\theta)\\\\\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)&=\\sin(180^{\\circ}+\\theta)\\\\\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)&=-\\sin\\theta.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q94-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, write down a singular expression for $\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)$ that shows all possible results.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p17-q94-c-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)=(-1)^n\\sin\\theta",
                    "display": false
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p17-q94-c-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "(-1)^n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " is 1 for even ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and −1 for odd ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "n",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "For even $n$, $(-1)^n=1$; for odd $n$, $(-1)^n=-1$. Thus both preceding results are expressed by\n$$\\sin(180n^{\\circ}+\\theta)=(-1)^n\\sin\\theta.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      }
    ]
  },
